          %h1#vopnkas_principle Vopěnka's principle
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#statements"} Statements
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_vopnka_principle"} The Vopěnka principle
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#the_weak_vopnka_principle"} The weak Vopěnka principle
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relativized_versions_of_vopnkas_principle"} Relativized versions of Vopěnka’s principle
              %li
                %a{:href => "#motivation"} Motivation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Consequences"} Consequences
              %li
                %a{:href => "#settheoretic_notes"} Set-theoretic notes
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#first_versus_secondorder"} First- versus second-order
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#vopnka_cardinals"} Vopěnka cardinals
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#definable_counterexamples"} Definable counterexamples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Vopěnka’s principle
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+cardinal"} large cardinal
            axiom which implies a good deal of simplification in the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
          %p
            It is fairly strong as large cardinal axioms go: Its consistency follows from the existence of
            = succeed "s," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                huge cardinal
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/huge+cardinal"} ?
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+cardinal"} measurable cardinal
          %h2#statements Statements
          %h3#the_vopnka_principle The Vopěnka principle
          %p Vopěnka’s principle has many equivalent statements. Here are a few:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p The VP is equivalent to the statement:
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p The VP is equivalent to the statement:
            %p
              For every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
              sequence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi α
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Ord
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle M_\alpha | \alpha \in Ord\rangle
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} first-order structures
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinals"} ordinals
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha\lt\beta
              for which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_\alpha
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+embedding"} embeds elementarily
              into
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_\beta
          #ColimitsCoreflective.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p The VP is equivalent to the statement:
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \hookrightarrow C
              which is closed under
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective subcategory
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#AdamekRosicky"}> AdamekRosicky, theorem 6.28
            ).
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p The VP is equivalent to the statement:
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrantly+generated+model+category"} cofibrantly generated model category
              (in a slightly more general sense than usual) is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial model category
          %p
            This is in (
            %a{:href => "#Rosicky"}> Rosicky
            )
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p If one insists on the traditional stricter definition of cofibrant generated model category, then the VP still implies that these are all combinatorial. But the VP is slightly stronger than this statement.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_5 Theorem
            %p The VP is equivalent to both of the statements:
            %ol
              %li
                For every
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%28n%29-extendible+cardinal"} C(n)-extendible cardinal
              %li
                For every
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%28n%29-extendible+cardinals"} C(n)-extendible cardinals
          %p
            This is in (
            %a{:href => "#BagariaCasacubertaMathiasRosicky"}> BCMR
            ).
          %h3#the_weak_vopnka_principle The weak Vopěnka principle
          %p The Vopěnka principle implies the weak Vopěnka principle.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_6 Theorem
            %p The weak VP is equivalent to the statement:
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \hookrightarrow C
              which is closed under
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
          %p
            This is
            %a{:href => "#AdamekRosicky"} AdamekRosicky, theorem 6.22 and example 6.23
          %h3#relativized_versions_of_vopnkas_principle Relativized versions of Vopěnka’s principle
          %p
            Vopěnka’s principle can be relativized to levels of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%C3%A9vy+hierarchy"} Lévy hierarchy
            by restricting the complexity of the (definable) classes to which it is applied. The following theorems are from (
            %a{:href => "#BagariaCasacubertaMathiasRosicky"}> BCMR
            ).
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_7 Theorem
            %p
              For any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ge 1
            %ol
              %li
                There exists a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%28n%29-extendible+cardinal"} C(n)-extendible cardinal
              %li
                Every proper class of first-order structures that is defined by a conjunction of a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{n+1}
                formula and a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_{n+1}
                formula contains distinct structures
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                and an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+embedding"} elementary embedding
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\hookrightarrow N
          %p
            The “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=0
            case” of this is:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_8 Theorem
            %p
              For any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ge 1
            %ol
              %li
                There exists a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercompact+cardinal"} supercompact cardinal
              %li
                Every proper class of first-order structures that is defined by a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2
                formula contains distinct structures
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                and an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+embedding"} elementary embedding
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\hookrightarrow N
          %p
            Many more refined results can be found in (
            %a{:href => "#BagariaCasacubertaMathiasRosicky"}> BCMR
            ).
          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %p
            From a category-theoretic perspective, Vopěnka’s principle can be motivated by applications and consequences, but it can also be argued for somewhat
            = succeed "," do
              %em a priori
            %em large discrete categories
            are rather pathological objects. We can’t avoid them entirely (at least, not without restricting the rest of mathematics fairly severely), but maybe at least we can prevent them from occurring in some nice situations, such as full subcategories of locally presentable categories. See
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/29302/reasons-to-believe-vopenkas-principle-huge-cardinals-are-consistent/29473#29473"} this MO answer
          %h2#Consequences Consequences
          #ConsequenceForBousfieldLoc.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_9 Theorem
            %p The VP implies the statement:
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+proper+model+category"} left proper
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial model category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Mor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z \in Mor(C)
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
              of
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization+of+model+categories"} left Bousfield localization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi Z
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_Z W
              exists.
          %p
            This is theorem 2.3 in (
            %a{:href => "#RosickyTholen"}> RosickyTholen
            )
          #ConsequenceForReflectiveInfCatLoc.num_corollary
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p The VP implies the statement:
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally presentable (∞,1)-category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              a class of morphisms in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} localization
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              extsts.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By the facts discussed at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally presentable (∞,1)-category
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial model category
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization+of+model+categories"} Bousfield localization of model categories
              we have that every locally presentable
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#ConsequenceForBousfieldLoc"} above theorem
          %h2#settheoretic_notes Set-theoretic notes
          %h3#first_versus_secondorder First- versus second-order
          %p
            As usually stated, Vopěnka’s principle is not formalizable in first-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZF"} ZF
            set theory, because it involves a “second-order”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifier"} quantification
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+classes"} proper classes
            (“…there does not exist a large discrete subcategory…”). It can, however, be formalized in this way in a class-set theory such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NBG"} NBG
          %p
            On the other hand, it can be formalized in ZF as a first-order axiom schema consisting of one axiom for each class-defining formula
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            does not define a class which is a large discrete subcategory…” We might call this axiom schema the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Vopěnka axiom scheme
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/45602/can-vopenkas-principle-be-violated-definably"} this MO question
            and answer.
          %h3#vopnka_cardinals Vopěnka cardinals
          %p Unlike some large cardinal axioms, Vopěnka’s principle does not appear to be merely an assertion that “there exist very large cardinals” but rather an assertion about the precise size of the “universe” (the “boundary” between sets and proper classes). In other words, the universe could be “too big” for Vopěnka’s principle to hold, in addition to being “too small.”
          %p
            (The equivalence of Vopěnka’s principle with the existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%28n%29-extendible+cardinals"} C(n)-extendible cardinals
            may appear to contradict this. However, the property of being
            = succeed "-extendible" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(n)
          %p
            More precisely, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is a cardinal such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\kappa
            satisfies ZFC + Vopěnka’s principle, then knowing that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda\gt\kappa
            does not necessarily imply that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\lambda
            also satifies Vopěnka’s principle. By contrast, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\kappa
            satisfies ZFC + “there exists a
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+cardinal"} measurable cardinal
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\lambda
            for any
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda\gt\kappa
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            is the next greatest
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inaccessible+cardinal"} inaccessible cardinal
            after
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %p
            Relativizing Vopěnka’s principle to cardinals also raises the same first- versus second-order issues as above. We say that a
            %strong Vopěnka cardinal
            is one where Vopěnka’s principle holds “in
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\kappa
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\kappa
            %strong almost-Vopěnka cardinal
            to be one where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\kappa
            satisfies the first-order Vopěnka scheme. Then one can show, using the Mahlo reflection principle (see
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/45602/can-vopenkas-principle-be-violated-definably/46538#46538"} here
            again), that every Vopěnka cardinal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is a limit of
            = succeed "-many" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
          %h3#definable_counterexamples Definable counterexamples
          %p If Vopěnka’s principle fails, then there exist counterexamples to all of its equivalent statements, such as a large discrete full subcategory of a locally presentable category. If Vopěnka’s principle fails but the first-order Vopěnka scheme holds, then no such counterexamples can be explicitly definable.
          %p
            On the other hand, if the Vopěnka scheme also fails, then there will be explicit finite formulas one can write down which define counterexamples. However, there is no “universal” counterexample, in the following sense: if Vopěnka’s principle is consistent, then for any class-defining formula
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            does not define a counterexample to it. See
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/45602/can-vopenkas-principle-be-violated-definably/46538#46538"} here
            yet again.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The relation to the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
            is the contents of chapter 6 of
          %ul#AdamekRosicky
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Ad%C3%A1mek"}> Jiří Adámek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Rosick%C3%BD"} Jiří Rosický
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Locally+presentable+and+accessible+categories"} Locally presentable and accessible categories
          %p
            The relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+categories"} combinatorial model categories
            is discussed in
          %ul#Rosicky
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Rosick%C3%BD"}> Jiří Rosický
              ,
              %em Are all cofibrantly generated model categories combinatorial?
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.muni.cz/~rosicky/papers/cof1.ps"}> ps
              )
          %p
            The implication of VP on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+localization"} cohomology localization
            are discussed in the following articles
          %ul#RosickyTholen
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Rosick%C3%BD"}> Jiří Rosický
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Tholen"} Walter Tholen
              %em Left-determined model categories and universal homotopy theories
              Transactions of the American Mathematical Society
              %p
                Vol. 355, No. 9 (Sep., 2003), pp. 3611-3623 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/1194855"}> JSTOR
                ).
          %ul#BagariaCasacubertaMathiasRosicky
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carles+Casacuberta"}> Carles Casacuberta
                , Dirk Scevenels,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeff+Smith"} Jeff Smith
                %em Implications of large-cardinal principles in homotopical localization
                Advances in Mathematics
              %p Volume 197, Issue 1, 20 October 2005, Pages 120-139
            %li
              %p
                Joan Bagaria,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carles+Casacuberta"} Carles Casacuberta
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Rosicky"} Jiří Rosicky
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Definable orthogonality classes in accessible categories are small
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.2792"} arXiv
          %ul
            %li
              Giulio Lo Monaco,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Vopěnka’s principle in ∞-categories
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.04251"} arxiv:2105.04251
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+cardinal"} large cardinal
            strength of the weak Vopěnka principle is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Trevor M. Wilson
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Trevor+M.+Wilson"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The large cardinal strength of Weak Vopěnka’s Principle
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.00284"} arXiv
          %p The following paper shows that weak Vopěnka’s principle is indeed weaker than Vopěnka’s principle:
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Trevor M. Wilson
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Trevor+M.+Wilson"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Weak Vopěnka’s Principle does not imply Vopěnka’s Principle
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.09333"} arXiv
          %p Applications to localizations of presentable (∞,1)-categories are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carles+Casacuberta"}> Carles Casacuberta
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Javier+J.+Guti%C3%A9rrez"} Javier J. Gutiérrez
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homotopy reflectivity is equivalent to the weak Vopěnka principle
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21244"} arXiv
          %p
            Some discussion on Vopěnka’s principle in the context of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+function"} polynomial function
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lesbegue measurability
            is independent of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            occurs in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+E.+Hanson"}> James E. Hanson
              ,
              %em Any function I can actually write down is measurable, right?
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.02693"}> arXiv:2501.02693
              )
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/foundational+axiom"} foundational axiom
